Choice quotes from my notes on the affair:<p>&quot;I said that if OpenAI released its result in the way proposed I would go public with what happened. The reply was, &#x27;Why would you ruin your career?&#x27; I replied that I am an academic, and asked why he thought going public would ruin my career. The reply was, &#x27;If you don&#x27;t want me to be nice, then I don&#x27;t have to be nice.&#x27;&quot; (Sébastien Bubeck later apologized on X for the remark.)
 &quot;Sebastien twice asserted that he wanted Levent removed from authorship...&quot;
&quot;The second was that, after posting Euler, I alone write a paper presenting the Navier-Stokes result, acknowledging that an internal OpenAI model had resolved it.&quot;<p>&quot;I asked whether the model had been trained on, or had access to, our sessions in Codex, into which we had been putting all our drafts for the whole of this project. I was told the model did not look up user data. I asked again, about training, and I did not get an answer.&quot;
(OpenAI subsequently stated publicly that while unlikely, it could not rule out that de-identified data from their product usage improved the model.)

I, too, enjoyed the note.<p>&quot;... the few trusted mathematicians I consulted hold
extremely similar views on the right things to do in these circumstances (it is
occasionally possible for physicists and mathematicians to agree...)&quot;

These proofs really are quite huge. The proof is 1.3GB, ~15M lines of code:<p><pre><code>  mkdir -p riemann_proof &amp;&amp; cd riemann_proof

  # fetch parts 01–14
  for i in {01..14}; do
    curl -fSL -o lean.tar.gz.part$i https:&#x2F;&#x2F;alpo.ge&#x2F;lean.tar.gz.part$i || { echo &quot;failed: part$i&quot;; break; }
  done

  # join and decompress (creates .&#x2F;lean)
  cat lean.tar.gz.part{01..14} &gt; lean.tar.gz
  tar xzf lean.tar.gz

  # size, file count, line counts
  du -sh lean | cut -f1 | xargs printf &#x27;%s on disk (lean folder)\n&#x27;
  find lean -name &#x27;*.lean&#x27; | wc -l | xargs printf &#x27;%s .lean files\n&#x27;
  find lean -name &#x27;*.lean&#x27; -print0 | xargs -0 cat | wc -l | xargs printf &#x27;%s lines total\n&#x27;
  find lean -name &#x27;*.lean&#x27; -print0 | xargs -0 cat | grep -cv &#x27;^[[:space:]]*$&#x27; | xargs printf &#x27;%s non-blank lines\n&#x27;</code></pre>
